WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
Welcome to fascinating frontiers, episode 71,

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.360
it's May 16th, 2026.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.460
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.740
A NASA probe is borrowing Mars's gravity

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.280
to reach a metal asteroid no spacecraft has ever visited.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.420
The psyche spacecraft completed its Mars fly by this week

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.600
to gain speed without using fuel.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.980
It captured thousands of test images during the past

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.640
to verify its cameras ahead of the planned arrival

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.820
at a metal asteroid in 2029.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.000
The trajectory depends on precise timing

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.000
and detailed modeling of the planet's gravity fields.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.700
Missions like this reach distant targets more efficiently

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.160
by harvesting orbital energy from planets along the way.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.420
Now here's something that blew my mind when I looked into it.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.120
A spacecraft can pick up thousands of kilometers per hour

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.300
from a planet's gravity without firing its engines once.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.120
Imagine riding alongside the probe

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.580
as it dives toward Mars.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.320
The planet's pole accelerates the craft,

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.420
but because Mars itself is moving around the Sun,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.460
the spacecraft leaves with a higher speed relative to the Sun

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.680
than it arrived with.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.460
In the case of psyche, the boost adds roughly two kilometers per second,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.860
enough to shorten the journey to the asteroid belt by years.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.320
The geometry must be exact, arriving a few minutes early

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.220
or late changes the exit velocity dramatically.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.480
Yet scientists still cannot predict with complete certainty

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.820
how a planet's uneven mass distribution

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.860
will tug on the spacecraft during the closest approach.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.460
That lingering uncertainty keeps trajectory teams

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.520
refining their models right up to the moment of the flyby.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:46.280
From engineered gravity assists that open the outer solar system,

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.000
we turn to a newly discovered stellar system

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.200
that is rewriting expectations for how compact binaries behave.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.520
Astronomers identified ZTFJ007 plus 4804

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.000
as the first known system where a hot sub-dwarf

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.320
and white dwarf produce repeated dwarf nova outbursts.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.540
Data from the Zwicky transient facility and TESS

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.200
revealed the unexpected activity in this pairing.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.280
The discovery marks the initial observed case

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.180
of this behavior in such a system,

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.960
while new stellar phenomena continue to surprise observers.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.760
Another kind of milestone is heading into orbit

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.580
aboard a routine cargo flight.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.640
Commemorative semi-quince antennae coins

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.440
from the US Mint flew aboard the latest SpaceX cargo mission.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.180
The shipment also carried matching stamps

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:43.060
and NASA mission patches marking America's 250th anniversary.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.880
The payload highlights the ongoing use of commercial flights

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.800
for symbolic and cultural items.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.780
Symbolic cargo is one way nation celebrates spaceflight.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.280
Another is open discussion of engineering lessons learned

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:57.640
from past programs.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.920
Engineers at the Russian Star City facility

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.280
are comparing the N1 rocket's 30 engine failures

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.800
to starships current multi-engine development.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.680
Their discussion focuses on the remaining technical challenges

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.680
for vehicles that rely on large numbers of engines.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.220
The exchange highlights ongoing international interest

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:18.720
and heavy-lift reliability.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.780
Lessons from past rockets often surface in public talks

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.600
by those who have lived the challenges

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.260
of long-duration spaceflight.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.420
Former astronaut Scott Kelly will discuss

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.340
long-duration mission realities

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:35.100
and future exploration goals during an appearance in Adelaide.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.680
The event focuses on the physiological

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.360
and operational challenges of travel beyond low earth orbit.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.540
It offers rare public insight from someone

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.740
with extended experience aboard the International Space Station.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.560
Professional astronauts push the boundaries of observation.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.300
Sometimes the same wonders are captured

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.940
by dedicated amateurs using everyday tools.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.260
A stacked image of the Orion Nebula produced

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:03.940
with a real-me eight-phone and an 18 times telephoto lens

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.820
reached more than an hour of total exposure.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.360
The result shows the Nebula in surprising detail

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.540
from a boardal three or four site.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:16.680
The outcome demonstrates that accessible deep sky imaging

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.580
is now possible for non-specialist observers.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:23.040
Today's missions keep showing how clever trajectories

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.500
and steady data collection steadily open new corners

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:27.580
of the solar system.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.720
Keep an eye on further updates from the psyche mission

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.200
as it continues toward its asteroid target.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.420
That covers today's space and science news.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.120
Share this with a fellow space enthusiast

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.240
if you found it interesting.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.160
I'm Patrick and Vancouver, see you tomorrow.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.120
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.400
and editorial selection and analysis are my own.
